  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n   The previous algorithm uses a multi-phase optimization with SLSQP and coordinate descent, but it may get stuck in local optima. We introduce a two-phase optimization: \n1. Bayesian Optimization (BO) for global exploration using a Fourier series representation of the function h(x) to reduce dimensionality.\n2. Fast Coordinate Descent (CD) for local refinement on the grid.\n   The Fourier series handles the non-convexity better and explores the search space more effectively. BO is used for its global optimization capabilities on continuous representations, while CD is efficient for fine-tuning the discrete function.\n\n   Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: h(x) = Fourier series coefficients c \u2208 \u211d^(2*K)\n   - Objective: minimize C5 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) dx] represented by: dx * np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n   - Constraints: \n        \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u2192 enforced by projecting Fourier series coefficients.\n        0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2192 enforced by clipping and penalty in BO.\n   - Fourier series for h(x) on [0,2]: basis functions = [1, cos(kx), sin(kx)] for k=1,2,...,K (with K selected based on grid size)\n   - Transformation: Convert Fourier coefficients to grid samples via Fourier basis evaluation.\n\n   Implementation procedure:\n   1. Initialization:\n        if 'initial_h_values' in globals(): start from initial_h_values at n=224.\n        else: generate random Fourier coeffs for n=224 grid.\n   2. Fourier-to-grid projection:\n        Given K, compute grid samples h at each x_i = i*dx as:\n            h = F * c\n        where F is the Fourier feature matrix of size n_x x (2*K) and we include the constant term.\n   3. Bayesian Optimization:\n        - Use Gaussian Process (GP) to model the acquisition function.\n        - Space: [c_min, c_max]^(2*K)\n        - Objective: sampled C5 after projection to grid and clipping.\n        - Minimize: C5, maximizing the acquisition if we use probability of improvement.\n        - Number of samples: floor(0.3*budget) for initial GP fitting.\n   4. Coordinate Descent:\n        - Starts from the best Fourier coefficient candidate.\n        - Perform local CD on grid samples to polish.\n        - Reset constraints on each CD iteration.\n   5. Mapping to grid:\n        - After each GP-SLSQP poll, evaluate at grid points and compute C5.\n   6. Hyperparameters:\n        - K=6 (adapt based on grid: Coarse grids have lower K, finer grids have higher K)\n        - GP kernel: RBF\n        - Bounds: c \u2208 [-bounds, bounds] with bounds chosen from the BO exploration.\n\n   Search choices and variations:\n   - K-selection: \n        low: K=3 (n=224, 32, etc.)\n        medium: K=6 (n=224)\n        high: K=9 (n=512, if budget allows)\n   - Use a grid of Fourier basis functions maximum at the given resolution.\n   - Primary: Bayesian Optimization using SLSQP for exploration.\n   - Secondary: Coordinate Descent for exploitation.\n\n   Computebudget and robustness:\n   - Time allocation: 30% for BO, 70% for CD (if budget allows parallel phases).\n   - HMEF: Use early stopping in BO if no improvement.\n   - Grid transfer: Interpolate between consecutive grids (448, 896, 1792) using cubic interpolation.\n   - Validation: Always compute C5 on the final grid after rounding and projection.\n\n   Validation and return contract:\n    1. Check 0 <= h[i] <= 1 and sum(h)*dx == 1.\n    2. Compute C5 using np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')*dx.\n    3. Candidate must satisfy C5 < 0.380876.\n    4. Return the best candidate found, which must be feasible and computed within budget.",
